statistics and data are at the heart of  the fight against kofig 19. they were  used to justify  england's second lockdown and they'll  probably guide us when it's lifted as  well  we can talk now to the government's most  senior advisor on statistics  in diamond thank you very much for being  on the programme this morning  so i want to start with the question  that everyone wants to know the answer  to  how prevalent is coronavirus in the uk  right now and are there any signs that  the speed at which it's spreading  is beginning to slow well the good news  let's start with the good news the good  news is yes we  are seeing a slow down in the rate of  growth  that means we're still increasing uh and  we are now  in england at around about 1.25 per  thousand that means that one in 85  people in england  we believe have the virus in wales a  little less at 1 in 100 scotland  135 northern ireland one 105.  so yes we are continuing to increase  the rate we're increasing the numbers  but the rate of growth is slowing  can you give us any um detail on who it  is who  is most likely to have coronavirus and  whereabouts they might be  yes well in england we have huge uh  regional uh variation um  we have seen uh in the the northwest  northeast and in yorkshire  um steady increases although they have  tended to flatten off  in recent weeks a little it's  now seen increases in in the last week  in the east midlands west midlands uh  and and the southeast  and rates are lower although slightly  increasing in the rest of the country  and then where has that been happening  well uh amongst younger people those  in their teenagers and early adults was  where this wave  started it's now moved into all age  groups  although again to finish with some good  news the good news is that amongst those  teenagers and younger adults  we are seeing some reductions at the  moment  it really is so refreshing to hear that  at least there is a bit of good news it  feels like there's been so many  interviews over the last  nine months where all we've had is the  kind of gloomiest statistics possible  and given the fact that although as you  say it's still spreading the rate  is slowing slightly do you think we are  on track to leave the lockdown in  england on the 2nd of december oh  sophie that's the sixty-four thousand  dollar question and um  i would love to give you an answer um we  we moved into this new period of  restrictions just a short while ago what  you'd expect to see  in the initial period of restrictions is  a continued increase very simply  because these are infections that people  would have caught  before the restrictions started we will  be working tirelessly  over the next couple of weeks to to  monitor how  the restrictions are working in  impacting  on the pandemic and we have  towards the end of the restrictions to  to provide  all the very best data we can to the  government to  help ministers with what will clearly be  a very difficult decision  um it's interesting isn't it because  some of the statistics suggest that the  virus was already slowing  before we went into the lockdown you  know the r number for example which  measures how many people each infected  person passes the virus to  it's been falling since the middle of  october we know that it's one to 1.2 now  but of course there's a lag it measures  what was happening a couple of weeks ago  so if the rate was already falling did  we need to look down at all  well i think we need to recognize that  uh that the r  number which has let's be clear uh a  a degree of uncertainty around it uh in  any time  you know we look at one number but  there's always a lower and upper limit  remains above one when it remains above  one  it means that we are continuing to  increase  and let's be also clear in different  parts of the country  that the rates were pretty high already  so they're pretty high already and  they're still  continuing to increase albeit at a lower  level they're getting bigger  so i don't think there was too much um  debate that the government had a  difficult but made the right call  okay and now throughout the pandemic  we've become so used to these  press conferences where the government  shows us very detailed slides  um of data but there's been some  criticism about the data that they show  that it's quite hard to unpick  have you been concerned at all that the  government's been effectively  self-selecting the data  that it shows us to back up their  arguments no i i couldn't  um support that sophie i'd have to say  uh during the the first wave some of my  colleagues were  embedded into uh the heart of government  to  make sure that the quality  of the presentations that were made to  the public were at the highest level  i think it's incredibly important my  experience and my conversations  throughout this pandemic with government  has been a real  wish right across government for  openness and for transparency  we at the office international  statistics do everything  we can to to support that and some of my  colleagues  will now be going back into the heart of  government really to work on making sure  that the communication of statistics  that  the graphs you may see are at the  highest possible level  you said uh that that you were quite  confident with the first wave  but that your colleagues were only just  going back in have you had any concerns  about  the way that data's been presented  during the second week then  we we at the ins have been really  working tirelessly to make sure that  openness and transparency  is at the heart of what we uh  do we have a real policy at ons that we  only talk about data  which are in uh the public domain  and i know that that commitment to  openness and transparency goes right  across government  um okay the uk uk statistics authority  which you had said earlier this month  the use of data has not consistently  been supported by transparent  information being provided in a timely  manner  as a result there is a potential to  confuse the public and undermine  confidence  in the statistics can you explain what  some of those concerns are  sophia i i hate to um get involved in a  technical point but i think you're  referring to  um a some work by the regulator the  office for statistical  regulation which was with the chair  of the uk statistics authority sir david  norgrove  i am the national statistician  we believe it's really incredibly  important that the regulator is  independent  and the work you're quoting is from the  independent regulator  okay and one of the areas where um the  government hasn't been completely  transparent should we say is over  compliance it's been quite hard to  get any information on how many people  are following the rules which clearly uh  you know is absolutely critical in the  fight against clover19  do you have any uh data on compliance  that's a really really good question  another important one if i may say so  sophie  and yes we run a weekly um  opinions and lifestyle survey which  talks uh  about things like you know are you  wearing um  face masks it talks about uh social  isolation or indeed  are you meeting with other families and  what we find particularly in those tier  three  areas was a really high degree of  compliance  uh with the  regulations and the restrictions that  were imposed in those areas we found a  very very high proportion of people  wearing face masks now that not to say  that everybody  is and i think one of the things i would  say is it is incredibly important you  know i might say to you  95 of people are reporting  wearing face masks and that is a big  statistic that's that's the statistic we  have  but at the same time that still says  that five percent  are not and the five percent of a very  big number like the population of  england  is still a reasonably big number so we  need to be  absolutely tireless in promoting the  restrictions but the very good news here  is that from the data that we have um on  on the  on adherence there then  then people are really adhering to the  restrictions i would have to say  um that we have less information uh on  um proportions of people uh say  self-isolating and that's work that we  will be  trying to improve our data on in the  short  time really interesting stuff as you say  an important part of the battle against  kovid  i'm keen to talk to you uh just before  we end about the economy the ons of  course  measures uh economic growth and we've  seen some quite encouraging figures more  recently  but of course we're now in in a lockdown  and how  concerned are you about the economic  recovery  well we've all got to be concerned about  the economic recovery let me start by  saying that the long-term  future of the economy is inextricably  linked  with getting on top of the pandemic and  that's that  i think that's the key thing but yes we  have seen three months  of growth in our gdp but again  it's been mixed uh it wouldn't be a  surprise  to you if i said look where we've seen  growth uh in the economy  has been in places like couriers and  computer repair  in in retail driven of course uh by  online  but with at the same time really uh  still major  um slow downs relative to to february  in transport in tourism and in the  creative industries one of the jewels in  our crowd  which are down uh 61 percent on their  that their place in february so the  position on the economy i would have to  say  is mix yes we have had some growth but  it is in  uh different sectors um and i would have  to say  that we have around seven hundred  thousand people uh  less uh on the payroll uh now than  we did uh yeah i'll pass on the payroll  on payrolls  uh now uh than we did in  february um but at the same time  uh there have been uh our our reading  of the number of vacancies is that they  did increase  although in the most recent period they  they have plateaued a little bit  so so it's a very mixed uh story on the  economy  but key to getting the economy back  flowing is getting on top of the  pandemic  okay uh certain diamond always really  good to talk and thank you so much for  coming the program this morning  always good to see you sophie

